L263: In NQ and NQ-Swap benchmarks, our ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ also demonstrates improvements over other benchmarks (Table cite100†2 ). ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ yields the highest EM and F1 scores on NQ and competitive results on NQ-Swap (More extensive results are shown Table  cite101†11 Appendix  cite31†C.3 ). Moreover, our method outperforms in truthfulness evaluation in both NQ and NQ-Swap benchmarks. In the multiple-choice task, our decoder consistently improves MC1 scores across all models (Table cite99†1 ).
L264: Notably, our decoder gains 3-6% improvements across different models in MC1. For the MC2 and MC3 metrics, our proposed method also demonstrates superior performance, wining over all decoders for all the models except Qwen-2.5-14B model. Table cite102†3 presents the performance of different decoding methods on the summarization tasks.SAMSum and XSum both benchmarks require understanding the context properly and summarizing it without hallucinations.
L265: ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ decoder achieves the best performance on both truthfulness and FActScore outperforming the baseline in both SAMSum & XSum benchmark. While maintaining competitive ROUGE-L scores, ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ significantly improves factual accuracy without sacrificing summary quality. A more detailed performance analysis of different parameter effects on these benchmarks are shown in Table cite103†10 Appendix cite30†C.2 L266: MBPP benchmark requires both factual accuracy and reasoning capability to handle the complex task. As shown in Table cite104†4 , our decoding method boosts accuracy (+6.73% gain over baseline) on programming task tasks over other benchmarks. These results highlights the adaptability of our proposed decoder across diverse tasks and LLMs.
L267: ### 5.1 Effect of Self-Information Gating
L268: We experiment our decoding with and without self-information gating. Table cite105†5 compares ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ and CoCoA (without self-informating gating) on TruthfulQA generation task for Llama-3-8b with $\alpha=1.0$. ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ with ConMLDS outperforms CoCoA ConMLDS and achieves around 1-4 percentage point improvement over base CoCoA method on $T\times I$ score. The benefit of gating is also evident when we consider samples without rejected answers.
L269: Without rejected samples, both fMLDS and ConMLDS variants of ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ outperforms CoCoA. Because of the superiority of ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ over CoCoA, we conducted most of our experiment with ${\rm CoCoA}_{\rm SIG}$ .
